Cabbberants bubbbled in Kunovice, chemists don't add to it
More than a Thousand tons of sour cabbage want to be produced in Slovácko Fruta Kunovice, which belongs to the Hamé food group in the coming months.
The season began here in October and will last about half a year.
Since 1925 he has been producing a healthy delicacy here in the traditional way, i.e. pedaling directly in large tanks.
In modern industrial times, it is a rarity.
Our workers tread almost a ten-kil-kilometre-metre route per shift.
Each of the ten tanks can accommodate fifteen to thirty tons of cabbage, the fulfillment of one takes three shifts,” said David Kladroba, director of the Slovácká Fruta plant.
Cabbage heads are transported directly from their fields by farmers mainly from Ostružsko and Břeclav.
Fruta employees then purify the vegetables, remove bristles and throw them into a curler.
From there, already sliced cabbage falls into swimming pools at a depth of six-metre, at which pedals dressed in white clothing and boots work tirelessly.
This discloses undesirable air.
The last trampled layer of cabbage is overlaid with a tarpaulin and loaded with water.
"The cabbage haember then matures six to eight weeks for spontaneous lactic fermentation without any addition of fermentation-promoting substances," emphasized the Kladruby director.
The natural process corresponds to the production in domestic conditions, the individual strata workers sprinkle with salt and cumin.
Cabhes after fermentation goes to the filling line.
It is packaged in a sweet-sour brine into sachets or jars and is subjected to sterilization.
Hamé supplies sour cabbage mainly to the domestic and Slovak markets.
It also registers interest in some other countries, such as the United Kingdom, Ireland and Canada.
