Frontier Airlines prepares to charge for hand luggage
Frontier Airlines plans to charge an up to $100 on its flights for storing hand luggage on board.
Frontier Airlines plans to introduce a fee of up to $100 for hand luggage and $2 for coffee or soda, although in their announcement on Wednesday they said passengers would be able to keep an entire can.
The new hand luggage fee refers to luggage in the upper storage compartment, so that small luggage under the seat will continue to be free of charge.
Frontier says it will charge $25 if the fee is paid in advance and $100 if passengers with a payment will wait until they arrive at the gate.
Frontier spokeswoman Kate O'Malley said a hundred-dollar fee should encourage passengers to take care of payment in advance.
"We don't want to collect this fee," she said.
Airlines started to charge for the first and second bags in 2008.
Travellers tried to avoid these charges and stuffed in hand luggage hidden in the storage compartment as much as they could, so there was often no room in this area.
Charges are one of the ways to teach passengers to take fewer things on board.
O'Malley has said the purpose of this new fee really isn't to raise more money.
The point is that Frontier's most loyal customers have made it quite clear that it is becoming more and more uncomfortable to find a place in the upper storage area.
Travellers who buy tickets on the company's website will not pay.
This means that one queue at Frontier’s gate can bring luggage for which they do not pay anything, while the other queuing passenger pays $100 $100 in a queuary queuing.
O'Malley said websites and check-in procedures will be modified to ensure that passengers learn about the fee before coming to the gate.
Frontier's new fee for hand luggage will not yet be in force until the summer, but the date has not yet been set.
Travelers often complain about baggage fees and other fees, but airlines love them.
He argues that the handling of luggage costs the money, and the passengers who demand this service should pay for it.
On Wall Street, many perceive an additional baggage fee as a sign that airlines are trying to collect enough money to cover the cost of air traffic after years of losses.
However, the majority did not reach the fees for hand luggage.
Spirit Airlines was the first to charge for hand luggage three years ago, and another low-rise company Allegiant Air later ensued an example.
The only company that also has such fees is the Hungarian Wizz Air, according to aviation consultant Jay Sorensen, who carefully monitors additional charges.
In his December 2011 report, he estimated that Spirit’s hand luggage fee would yield $50 million a year.
Sorerensen, a former Midwest Airlines executive, recently flitted with Spirit and marveled at what he had seen at the gate when passengers had encountered Spirit's unusual charge for hand luggage.
"Getting on a plane was the smoothest I've ever seen in my aviation career," he said.
Expecting I'd see a gnashing of teeth and quarrels at the gate.
The plane was full, he said, "and the on-board took place in an instant."
Frontier will also apply fees for coffee, tea, soda and Juice fees.
Frontier stated that passengers who get lemonade or mushuania can keep an entire can and the coffee will be replenished free of charge.
Water will continue to be served free of charge.
U.S. Airways briefly attempted to introduce beverage charges in 2008, but after compliments by passenger complaints seven months later it retreated, and no other major airline mimiliated it.
Frontier’s latest move to charge for hand luggage, if passengers do not purchase a flight directly away from the airline, is its last effort to direct customers directly to its website.
The airlines pay online travel resellers such as Orbitz from $10 to $25 for each ticket sold.
This prompted all air companies to encourage passengers to make purchases directly away from them, instead of doing it through online travel agencies.
However, Frontier went the furthest in this area.
In September she began to give customers who bought through an online travel agency, just half a mile as part of a loyalty program.
On Wednesday she trimmed a mitre for 25 per cent millennium.
So the journey with Frontier 1000 miles long purchased through an online travel agency means a 250-kilometre reward.
The company also allows customers to choose their sedan in advance only if they make a purchase directly through Frontier’s website.
Frontier has a loyal customer base in its hometown of Denver, but its business is beginning to blunt and lose money.
According to the financial results presented on the Wednesday corporate partner Republic Airways Holdings, sales fell by 9 per cent and transport capacity decreased by almost 13 per cent in the first quarter
By the sale of this airline, Republic is also trying to solve the financial problems of Frontier.
It doesn’t accedine hackers for crashing an NSA website, but ‘internal error’
Mysterious National Security Agency reported last Friday that its website crashed for several hours last week because of an internal error, not because of an attack by hackers, as some claimed on the Internet.
