Kenyan press outraged by Controversiture Media Act
"It's scary news and it's appropriate to ask what will prevent parliament from simply swept the independence of the judiciary tomorrow," the newspaper said, considering the bill unconstitutional.
"This law is strict and very repressive, and we deny it," said Cyrus Kakau, CEO of Capital Group, including CapitalFM, one of the most acquaintance independent radio stations and news sites.
He said the new media tribunal "will always be biased because it's an overblown hand of the government" and that restrictions on content and advertising would damage Kenya's position in the global economy.
"I hope that the president will listen to us, and we will ask him to reject this bill and return it to MPs," he said.
According to the Star daily, the bill actually passes on “complete control of the media” to the government, and Standard newspapers state that democracy and freedom of expression in Kenya “worshiped a hard blow” and criticize the bill as “strict.”
Approval of the bill arrives amid a series of measures aimed at boosting national security immediately queues after an Islamic shooter's attack at the Westgate shopping center in September.
Invoicing of the authorities reversed Kenyan media when it aired a footage of a security camachine, on which soldiers sent to the site of the attack allegedly looted a festive convenience store.
The police director responded by summoning two reporters and the head of the media worker for questioning, but the subpoena was later withdrawn after media pressure.
According to the new draft law, media companies can be fined up to $20 million by horse-shillings and individual journalists up to one million, along with the “exemplification from the list” risk, or an official journalistic accreditation may be denied.
The tribunal also has the right to confiscate the property of the perpetrator as long as the fine is not paid.
