They have to save, young Czechs know.
But most of them don't even have any interest
Increasing Czechs are set aside for money.
Nevertheless, they do not reach sausage champions in the region.
Most of the money is set aside by Austrians, who save an average of €181 per month.
A little more than the Czechs also save people in Slovakia.
This is according to an Erste Savings Barometer group in Central and Eastern Europe.
It also turned out that the mood of savers is improving, and two-thirds of respondents are planning that they will set aside more money.
While the Czechs are roughly in the middle of a ranking of savers, in Romania and Serbia people still use very little savings-making products.
For example, according to the poll, almost every second Serb saves at home "under a pillow".
In the Czech Republic this year, there has been an increase in the number of those who save higher amounts per month.
Last year, 26 percent of respondents were debuffided every month for various types of savings, this year it is five per cent more.
However, the average monthly deposit in the year-on-on-on-Rar comparison fell slightly to 2078 crowns.
The biggest hit among savings products is pension insurance.
54% of Czechs prefer this, while three years ago it was the most popular way to evaluate savings for only 36 percent of people.
Conversely, the popularity of building savings is declining, compared to the previous year, interest in investment funds has also fallen significantly.
Only 16 per cent of respondents already use them, compared to almost a quarter last year.
The survey also turned out that although people find it important to save, most of them don’t know what yields they achieve on individual products.
Above all, young people under the age of 30 do not know how their money is interest-bearing.
Conversely, the oldest group of respondents has the best awareness about the profitability of their deposited money, 73% of respondents know it among people over fifty.
In the international comparison of the Erste group, Czechs lead in the use of on-line banking, which is regularly used by 44% of people.
