UN Celebrates New Difficulties Fight against Poverty
The United Nations is beginning to work urgently on a new set of goals that will replace the Millennium Development Goals (MDGs), which were introduced 12 years ago in order to collude with global poverty.
Awaiting the United Nations Summit on Sustainable Development, which began at Rio de Janeiro last night, Australian diplomats played a key role in promoting “Sustainable Development Goals” designed to rebuild MDG, which will expire in 2015.
They were included in the final concept of the document, which will be endorsed by world leaders, including Mrs Gillard, during the summit.
U.N. secretary-general Ban Ki-moon said overnight at the summit that it is now time to “elevate ourselves above national interests.”
"I am pleased that Member States have agreed to launch and take over a process designed to put in place the general Sustainable Development Goals SDG," he said.
SDGs will build on the progress achieved under the Millennium Development Goals and will be an integral part of the post-2015 development programme.
I'm going to make every effort to fulfil the mandate given to me by the Member States and to realize our vision of the Sustainable Development Goals, which builds on MDG's success.
