Renamo was once an iniquitably well-known insurgent movement, originally backed by the white government in Rhodesia and then by the rule of apartheid in South Africa as part of efforts to destabilise an independent government.
After the peace agreement in 1992, it became an opposition party.
Analytists are confident it is unlikely that an open-out conflict should break out again in the country, but recent events have frightened foreign investors and locals.
Fast-growing economy is at stake, as discovering vast offshore reserves of crude oil and coal deposits in the north-sweather can bring investments worth more than $550 billion over the next few years.
