He told the FT newspaper that he meant the Frelimo party was no longer respecting the deal.
"Our goal is to go back to the negotiations, but with seriousness," Mr Mazanga said.
Previous talks between these parties have done little to easing tensions between these parties, fueled by a series of skirmishes this year.
It is a skirmitory between two great personalities (Guebuza and Dhlakama), the lecturer at the Open University and Mozambique expert Joseph Hanlon.
Neither of them is a good negotiator, and neither of them is ready to make the necessary concessions.
Renamo, which sought electoral changes, has already stated that it would boycott the municipal elections scheduled for November.
Presidential and parliamentary elections are planned for next year.
Some commentators have misinterpret the use of force as an attempt by the moribund movement to obtain benefits and financial proceeds from the government.
Renamot's electoral share has been delegated since 1992, while a more recent party, the Mozambique (DHM Democratic Movement), which was founded by a former Renamo party member, is expected to improve its position in the polls.
Mr Mazanga says Mr. Guebuza - whose second term ends next year - wants to destroy the country's democracy.
"He doesn't want a democratic system of multiple parties, he doesn't want transparent elections, he doesn't want peace because he doesn't want the presidency," Mazanga said.
It's uncleatable what the Renamo party's options have, but he has carried out divisive terrorist attacks on police stations and vehicles on a key road between the north-and-south.
Most skirmishes have taken place in the province of Sofala, which, although several hundred kilometers away from the capital Maputo, but where the city of Beira, the port on which the mining companies, including Rio Tinto and Vale, in its exports of coal.
In June, Rio's company paused for about a week's use of the railways when Renamo had threatened to attack the line.
Mr Mazanga didn't want to comment on whether Renamo would repeat his threat.
Renamo wanted to "warn an international community that Mozambique situation isn't good," Mazanga said.
Instability has increased frustration from the government, says Fernando Lima, the head of an independent Mediacoop media company, and many people are also concerned about corruption, the slow pace of development and the recent flood of kidnapping.
"People think that responsibility for the future of the country lies solely with the government and the president, and that he should be the one who finds a solution to the problems," he says.
Demonstration Omar Sultuane says that people just want stability.
“Neither Renamo nor Frelimo is interested in anyone, people just want peace again, they want free access to the roads,” he says.
