The Brno observatory will take visitors into space in a new digitium
At first glance, it is evident that the hall of the Great Planetarium has changed over the past six months.
There is a new stage, visitors can sink into the new comfortable seats and above their heads there has added a 17-metre spruce-shaped spruce area from the American company Spitz.
In the avenues is its most important part, a new projector from Japan.
In order for the image to be perfect, other smaller projectors are scattered across the hall.
Another important part of the whole device is hidden behind the walls of the hall.
"There are twenty-one computers that generate an image," said Jiří Dušek, director of the Brno observatory.
Computers will ensure that the image from several projectors does not overlap, but gives a complete impression.
"Compute performance is thirty to forty times higher than for conventional personal computers," said Tomáš Hladík, a technician from the observatory.
Digitárium can conjure up an exceptionally realistically and plastic-like model of the nocturnal sky, the human body or the bottom of the ocean.
Spectrums find themselves right in the middle of a person's universe.
Another similar device is closest in Warsaw or Hamburg.
The observatory’s management plans to project the purchased programs and its own work in the hall.
There have been two shows on the agenda since Saturday.
The first is called Astronaut and is intended for adults.
People will learn about the training of cosmonauts, flitted into space, turn to centrifuges, and then on illustrative examples they will see how demanding conditions astronauts in the cosmos face.
The second show is for the children and it is called The Magic Reef - Kaluoka'hina.
It tells the story of a clerk whose colorful inhabitants lived a peaceful life up to the moment when a nearby volcano elapsed.
The entire ditoria cost about fifty million crowns, which was paid mainly by the Ministry of Education.
The city of Brno and the observatory from its budget also contributed smaller amounts.
The projection area cost ten million crowns, the projector twelve million.
"The rest was swallowed by audio-visual equipment and computers," Dušek explained.
The installation of the digitarium followed the reconstruction of the entire building, which the observatory underwent two years ago.
According to director Dušek, 25 to 30 thousand visitors could still come by the end of the year.
We have studied 11 THOKS OF ADS!
We know where they want you at work!
In November of last year, the analysis captured more than 11,000 advertisements offering jobs, of which 4,300 were published in the daily newspaper and 6,800 ads (62 percent) on the Internet.
Therefore, the Internet becomes the most important medium for the publication of job offers.
Even in 2005, when the poll was conducted for the first time, it accounted for 26 per cent.
In the last two inquiries (2007, 2010), it has stabilised at around 60 per cent.
According to the analysis, the advertising of job opportunities significantly reflects the current situation on the labour market.
An example is a simple comparison of the number of published advertisements in a period of economic prosperity and in times of economic crisis.
For example, almost 25,900 ads were published in the same reference period in 2007, in 2012 it was less than half.
The offer of positions on the Internet is oriented towards a narrower, especially a more qualified group of professions.
On the other hand, the offer of employment in the daily newspaper covers a wider range of professions, including workers’ ones, the author of the analysis Pavlína Šťastnová said.
Representation of selected professional groups varies in both media.
On the internet, advertisements looking for managers and executives, sales representatives, programmers and financial experts have taken the leading positions.
The position of programmers or heads of small businesses only featured on the Internet.
In the daily press, the share of these professional professions is significantly lower, on the contrary, a large representation belongs mainly to service and workers' professions.
The most frequently cited requirements, which employers express through an advertising offer include, of course, education.
Other monitored areas included the knowledge of foreign languages, especially English.
In the day-to-day press, the requirement for knowledge of a foreign language was given in three percent of advertisements, on the Internet in 37 percent.
Only in two per cent of advertisements, employers demanded an active proficiency of two foreign languages.
Demands on 'computer literacy' appeared in 40 percent of adverts (in day-digestion in 11 per cent, on the Internet in 57 percent).
The practice in the field was claimed in 54% of advertisements.
Practition requirements are mostly associated with skilled professions.
In particular, production managers and other executives.
Worker's Professions mainly include mechanics and repairers or manufacturers and processors of food products or repairers of electrical appliances, the author of the study Jiří Tillner added.
Employers also indicate the requirements for the characteristics and skills of potential candidates in half of their advertisements.
As the most frequently required skills, communication skills appear in the first place for a long period of time.
Employers also require flexibility, adaptability and dynamism, autonomy, responsibilities, organizational skills and reliability.
The Tenth Anniversary of the New York Comedy Festival: The New Yorker
It could be argued that New York City is the place where solo comic performances were born in America: almost a hundred years ago, the variety artist Frank Fray, who worked as a premiership of ceremonies at the Palace Theatre theatre on Broadway, began to tell the assembled people with jokes using an interview method.
Faya's innovation has expanded over the years, most recently through New York's comedy festival.
Founded and directed by Caroline Hirsch, the founder of the so-Solo comedy's institution Caroline, celebrates its tenth anniversary this year - along with more than sixty performances in small clubs and major theaters.
