George Kerevan: Distributed Europe gives Scots a choice
Another day, another haunting story of independence.
This time, there is a warning that the condition for the membership of an independent Scython in the EU would be his accession to Schengen.
This misleevolved a cause for stories of passport checks in Berwick and barbed-wire along Hadriana's ramparts.
It is true that Strathclyde newspapers mentioned the possible economic advantages of free movement with the rest of Europe, but – as might be expected – this was not reflected in newspaper headlines.
Also, no one pointed out that EU Member States spend a lot of their time adapting their official laws as long as it suits them.
Since Scotland isn't in Schengen right now, an offer of continued inconsistency would be a cheap concession for Brussels in return for anything they really want from the Scots.
So it's a pretty weak story.
Moreover, so old that it has become an excavation. A journalistic duck that “independence means passport controls” I heard at least 40 years ago.
Nevertheless, there is an interesting idea in this whistleful story.
Why should an independent Scotland be expected to obey Europe?
So why exchange a London choke for Brussels, especially right now?
This is the real European story: the big post-war plan to unify Europe has finally failed.
Influence of the euro crisis is the so-called Europe Project officially dead.
Across across Europe, the influence of the parties are initiating opposition to the EU or the abolition of the euro as a common currency.
Even in Germany the Euro-Skeptical Alternativa party for Germany – founded this year – came from nowhere and has already captured nearly five million votes in the September federal elections, essentially “sweaping” the Free Democrat (equivalent to the British Liberal Democrats).
There has always been an opposition to the plan to create a federal Europe.
However, the current economic crisis has become a decisive moment.
Satisfaction dictated by Germany and the European Central Bank, together with straitjacket-brought tied to national economies through clinging to a common currency, have led many people to believe that the Europe project went too far.
The crisis of the euro has little to do with the fact that national governments manage a high budget deficit – it was only true in the case of Greece.
Rather, it’s a system of the euro that blocks its members at exchange rates advantageous for German exporters – and German politicians want to keep that.
Without the possibility of devaluing the domestic currency, southern Europe has a downturn compared to Germany.
The only help is to cut incomes and public spending – which is what Germany encourages.
Behind the current problems with the budget and the currency, there is a deeper cause of European malaise performance.
As a result of the “green” energy policy backed by Brussels – a code for supporting French and German companies at the expense of consumers – European industry pays twice as much for electricity and four times more for gasoline than it does in the United States.
This is a paralysing cost disadvantage, as we have already seen in Grangemouth.
Not even any salary freezes in the world would avert the seizing of the European petrochemical industry with cheap shale crude crude oil from the U.S.
As a surviving rebellion is a smoupling rebellion, especially in France, an enthusiastic supporter of the EU.
After the post-vulsion, the political elite in France saw the EU as an instrument that Germany would keep at bay and allow Paris to stand on the level of Washington in the world.
But Berlin no longer needs Paris as a political legitimacy card and promoted its own economic policy in Europe and left the worn-out French economy to its fate.
Responsibility: Marine Le Pen's right flank, anti-European National Front, has just just won a crucial extra election and relegated the ruling Socialists in third place.
The queue, with 24% of the vote, is now the most popular party in France – an erroneous warning to British Labour that they cannot conclude that fracturing of the right will automatically positively impact on the left.
How did Le Pen deal with her newfound popularity among the white working class?
Next year’s European Elections would like it to create an anti-European bloc operating against a common currency across the European Parliament.
If the anti-European parties do well in these elections, which is probable, such a bloc may prevail for the first time in the history of the European Parliament.
It occurs to me that in the foreseeable future the growing anti-European feeling and stagnation against the common currency will come together and the euro will perish.
The EU does not go away, but rather will be transformed into something looser in the sense of “Europe (Sovereign) nations”, which was favored by General de Gaulle.
Germany and several of its satellite economies may retain the euro, but France and southern Europe will resume their own currencies.
I'm expecting the United Kingdom to distancing itself from this project and stick to the United States.
But Washington's increased interest in the Pacific suggests that Britain will be retained atlantic refrigeration.
And where will Scotland be?
We can choose to be an area (in principle) of Little England.
Or we can hinder our own economic interests – this also includes the opportunity to announce to Berlin and Brussels, when they should let us go.
Assume that Scotland would do well in Europe's vacancies, assemblished by retaining its own currency.
Collaboration with other like-minded countries would be simpler in a non-federal Europe of nations.
Otherwise, we should take an example from Norway and maintain our economic independence.
The SNP government in Scotland is – odd surprisingly – the most successful political movement not promoting aussature measures in Europe, which was won by a large-suprement in 2011 thanks to the opposition stance against the cuts planned (and implemented) Labour Chancellor Alistair Darling and the following coalition of Conservatives and the Liberal Democrats.
It would be ridiculous for Scotland to now vote for independence only to take austerity dictated by Berlin-Brussels.
Early Puberty: Advanced Earlier
Research has shown that African-Americans and Hispanics reach puberty earlier than their Caucasian controversy.
Physical changes don't mean puberty is approaching
It is not proven that there are hormones or other chemicals behind it
Experts argue that one of the triggers of an earlier puberty may be an epidemic of obesity.
Earlier puberty is not so noticeable for boys.
Former CNN correspondent Pat Etheridge is a journalist specializing in children's health and family issues.
Should the mother be horrified when her daughter's breasts begin to bloom and burrow hair at the age of 7 or 8?
Speaking this week in Orland, Florida, a pediatric endocrinologist Dr. Paul Kaplowitz explained that these physical repetitions are quite common among U.S. girls and represent a new standard.
"I've spent a lot of time making sure my parents isn't usually a sign of rapid development towards full puberty," Kaplowitz said.
Visible signs of development, such as breast growth, shaggy and understepping and bodily odors appear in girls earlier.
In the last forty years, however, there has only been a slight displacement at the age at which the first menstruation takes place.
In the U.S. it is now an average age of 12.5 years compared to the age of 12.75 years in 1970.
"When the breasts start to develop, at least two to three years will pass before the first menstruation takes place," said Kaplowitz, the author of "Early puberty in girls: The Essential Guide to Coping with This Common Problem" (The Necessary Guide to Managing This Frequent Problem)
The most reliable test of how puberty will continue is time.
Discusses what activates the actual beginning of puberty, but if breast growth is accompanied by growth spurt before the age of 8, the girl is considered “pretemporarily advanced”.
In most cases, the process is slowed down or stopped – which can be carefully monitored by pediatricians.
Faster growth may be the reason for an endocrinologist to perform tests in order to eliminate serious problems, such as a tumor or a cyst.
There are treatments that delay the early menstruation and prevent further consequences: premature aging of bones, which can eventually lead to a cessation of growth and dnate in adulthood.
Recommendations about drugs and hormonal treatment are dependent on the age of the child, the speed of development, the speed of growth and emotional ripeness.
Psychosociological aspectations are also important.
Receipt when it comes to taking drugs, Kaplowitz is cautious, but recognizes that "suppressing puberty can alleviate the girl's behavior problems and her feelings that she differs from her peers."
Ofcomprehensible is another big problem: Parents simply don’t want their very young daughters to have menstruation.
"They're worrying that he can get pregnant or how he's going to handle hygiene," Kaplowitz said.
“It was a shock,” recalls a woman whose daughter started having menstruation at the age of 10.
Although there were hints and we talked about menstruation, she wasn't prepared emotionally.
She came home from school, frightened and angry, that she was first of her friends.
There are many well-familiar theories about the causes of premature puberty.
However, there is not yet a coherent set of evidence that hormones in milk or other foods, chemicals in the environment or sexual message in the media are to blame.
Boys – like girls – reach puberty earlier.
Kaplowitz asserts the presumption that the most serious reason is an epidemic of obesity.
In 2001, he assisted with conducting a study on girls between the ages of 6 and 9, which connects body fat with the timing of puberty.
Further findings support this conclusion, but there are many other contributing factors.
In this country, African-Americans and Hispanics have an inclination to reach puberty earlier than their Caucasian counterparts.
Explanations are different.
From a global point of view, early puberty patterns appear to be influenced by everything from economic conditions through the climate to genes.
Another quarreling: Although the boys appear at a young age with facial and murmuring, the trend towards the full bloom of early puberty isn't as noticeable as it's for girls.
Other physicians attending the American Children's Doctors (AAP) conference have added more complexities to this topic.
Acne and shaggy are common even in infants and toddlers.
"We have to be careful when it comes to how we identify the actual onset of puberty," said Lawrence Silverman, a Paediatric Endocrinologist at Goryeb Children's Hospital in Morristown New Jersey.
Parents do not hesitate to get instructions from their pediatrician on how to talk to their child.
“It can mean having to talk to a child sooner than you’d have anticipated,” Kaplowitz said.
If you stay calm, your child is likely to react well.
The girls who evolve earlier need reassuantment that even if this is done prematurely, this process is a normal part of life.
