Negotiant Vaccine Can Take away Smoking Pleasure
Scientists have developed a nicotine vaccine, which can take away the pleasure of smoking a cigarette.
A single dose of the vaccine has been able to protect the mice against nicotine addiction for a lifetime.
Ahead of human trials, it is necessitated to carry out additional tests that will take a few years, but Professor Ronald Crystal of the Weill Cornell medical school in New York says the first indications are good.
"We firmly hope that this kind of vaccination strategy can finally help the millions of smokers who have attempted to stop and have exhausted all the methods available on the market today, but they've found their nicotine reliance so strong enough to outweigh all current approaches," Professor Cornell said.
The new Vaccine contains a harmless virus, which has been assembled in such a way as to carry genetic information to create anti-nicotine antibodies.
The virus selectively infects the cells of the liver, which then begin to form a smooth stream of these antibodies.
Antibodies persecute all nicotine molecules in the bloodstream and neutralize them before they get into the brain, thus preventing the smoker from getting nicotine.
They continued their normal activities during the tests of vaccinated mice, to which nicotine was subsequently delivered.
But mice that weren't vaccinated were "cooled," the researchers said, a sign that nicotine got into their brain.
The experiments are copied in the journal Science Translation Medicine.
The previous tobacco vaccines have failed because they contained antibodies.
Injections had to be given so often to maintain sufficient antidote levels, thus proving expensive and impractical.
The cost of the new vaccines should be much lower, as they are converting liver cells into factories for the production of antibodies.
Professator Crystal said that if future vaccines are completely safe for people, they could be given to children before they want to try a cigarette, avoiding nicotine addiction.
However, it would be much more likely to be used by smokers to stop.
"They're going to know that if they start smoking again, they won't get any benefit from it because of a nicotine vaccine, and that's going to help them get rid of this bad habit," he said.
British scientists have said that the results were interesting, but they point out that further research is needed.
