Is the European elite ready to trade with the United Kingdom?
The Business for Britain campaign was launched in April with a promise to connect businesses and define what changes in the relationship with the EU British entrepreneurs and job-creating companies want to see.
For this purpose, we have commissioned the largest and most comprehensive survey, in which leading British entrepreneurs questioned their views on Britain, trade and the EU.
YouGov inquired 1,000 leading entrepreneurs representing British companies of various sizes and from different sectors and regions.
The results of the survey will come as a surprise.
We have found that the vast majority of businesses are now trying to export outside Europe and are concentriving on countries that are modernizing and growing while EU countries are stagnating.
They want to see the government prioritizing new trade ties with countries such as China, India and Brazil rather than getting bogged down into a long and strenuous process of responsoring mysterious European institutions.
Regarding their views on specific policy areas – from monopolistic regulations to product laws, most entrepreneurs expressed the opinion that controlling these key competencies should return to Westminster.
There was general dissatisfaction with the unified market, and entrepreneurs said that the costs of Brussels regulations now predominated the benefits of being part of the European business area – even 40 percent of large enterprises, traditionally the most pro-European companies, agreed.
Ultimately, our reconnaissance business leaders revealed the most eloquefy, namely that a definiful majority wants Britain to strive to change the treaty and the relationship with the EU, which should be based on trade, not politics.
It follows from the recognizability finding that the business sector is pushing “meamutorial change” that will bring its influence back to Britain.
There is a lot at stake – achieving a treaty change and a better agreement for Britain can mean a gain of up to 16 per cent when a referendum on remaining in the EU.
The premier of the prime minister should not hesitate: this survey shows that British trade supports his plan to amend the conditions of British EU membership.
It also demonstrates that the business sector expects that this amendment will mean a significant shift away from the simultaneous balancing of forces backwards towards Britain.
A better deal for the UK is possible, and given how the eurozone is embarking on a journey beyond a narrower economic and fiscal union, even increasingly necessary.
The priority must be jobs and growth in the UK, and, as the results of our survey show, for entrepreneurship it means re-focusing on trade and a fundamental change in the regulatory approach of Brussels.
