Scientists shed more indicative of how tail movements are related to the mood of the dog
Earlier research has shown that joyful dogs wagging their tail more right (from the dog's point of view), while nervous dogs wave their tail mostly to the left.
Now, however, the researchers say that other dogs are able to record these subtle differences in the movement of the tail and react to them.
Professor Georgio Vallortigara, a neurobiologist at the University of Trento said: "It is well-known in humans that the left and right sides of the brain have different links to stimuli that evoke positive or negative emotions."
We tried to observe the same thing in other species.
He added that, as is the case in humans, the right-hand side of the brain is accountable for left-handed movements and vice versa and that these two hemispheres play a different role in emotions.
Experiencing further information on how dogs respond to asymmetrical wagging the tail of other dogs, the researchers monitored animals that watched films about other dogs.
They measured the heart rate of these animals and analyzed their behavior.
It probably won't be a long time before we understand why their tail sometimes goes on one side, and at other times on the other.
Professor Vallortigara said: "We have projected dog movies about dogs - whether in the natural version or in the form of a silhouette - to get rid of any confusing impulses, and we were also able to modify the movement of the tail and represent it more on the right or to the left."
When the animals saw an otherwise insignificant dog moving its tail to the right (from the point of view of a dog wagging its tail), they remained completely at rest.
However, when it recorded the rotation of the tail mostly to the left (again from the point of view of the dog wagging its tail), their heart rate increased and the animals seemed inseparable.
Professor Vallortigara has said he didn't think that dogs would consciously communicate with each other through these movements.
Rather, he thinks that the dogs have learned from the experience on which movements they have to and that they do not have to look out for.
He said: "If you encounter dogs several times and the movement of their tail in one direction is repeatedly associated with rather friendly behavior, and the movement of the traffic breeds less friendly behavior, you will react on the basis of this experience."
Investigators say the findings can help dog owners, veterinarians and trainers better understand the emotions of their animals.
Breeding expert John Bradshaw, a university veterinarian at the University of Bristol, said it wasn't the first study to look at whether the left and the right-hand side were important for dogs.
Last year, a team at Lincoln University found that dogs turned their heads on the to the left when watching an Aggressive dog and on the right as they look at a hilarious dog.
And in another expert treatise from Victoria University in Canada, he said: "With a canine robot, dogs were more likely acquainted themselves when they wagged their tails to the left, when they were rather inseparable - exactly compared to an Italian study."
According to him, the difference may be due to the fact that dogs in these various studios did not interpret animals in films or canine robots completely like dogs.
Explained that studies on how dogs respond to real dogs could help.
“While in the case of many different mammals, there is strong evidence that both sides of the brain are used for different purposes, many details still need to be clarified—and dogs aren’t an exception.”
But given how easy it is to record their behavior, it probably won won’t be long before we can figure out why their tail sometimes goes to one side, and other times on the other.
Arctic Monkeys postpone concert in Glasgow due to Alex Turner's illness
Rock band Arctic Monkeys postponed a concert in Glasgow after the lead singer was diagnosed with laryngitis.
The Sheffield group was due to perform on Friday in the city's Hydro complex.
Chief Scoundrel's illumination, Alex Turner, has forced her to reshutled.
Announcement of the group came after it was similarly forced to postpone Thursday's performance at the LG Arena in Birmingham.
In a statement on its official website, Arctic Monkeys says: "After the decision to postpone today's appearance at the LG Arena in Birmingham and on medical referenules, the Arctic Monkeys group must also postpone performances at the Hydro complex in Glasgow scheduled for Friday, November 1. "
"Alex Turner has been diagnosed with inflammation of the larynx, and unfortunately he won'n't be able to get out."
A performance at the LG arena in Birmingham will take place on November 20 and an appearance at the Hydro complex in Glasgow on November 21.
All tickets for these appearances remain in force.
We would like to apologise to everyone who has bought tickets for the inconvenience it caused them.
If you need further information, please contact the cash register customer service where you have purchased your tickets.
Pope Francis appoints first cardinals in February
The Vatican announced on Thursday that Pope Francis will first appoint new cardinals of the Catholic Church for the first time on February 22.
Cardinals have the highest position in clergy of the Catholic Church as soon after the pope and are the ones who vote for the pope, so that Francis will establish his first group of men, who will eventually help to select his successor.
There are now 201 cardinals.
However, as soon as the cardinal reaches the age of 80, he can no longer take part in the pope’s election – the group is thus shrinking to 120 voting cardinals.
Speaking in a statement announcing this report, Father Federico Lombardi, a spokesman for the Vatican, said a meeting of contemporary cardinals known as a conspiratorship would take place prior to ceremonial urging new cardinals.
“Pope Frantisek decided to communicate his decision to convene a conspiratorition in February in advance in order to facilitate the planning of other assemblies that require the participation of cardinals from different parts of the world,” Lombardi said.
Jack Valero of the Catholic Voices newspaper said the number of cardinals is likely to fall by February.
He argued that the pope usually appoints as many cardinals as it is necessary for the number of Electoral Electorals to rise again to 120, and so many cardinals over the age of the age of 80.
Consistory next year will be significant because it will be the first after Francis was elected in March this year, Valero said.
At the moment, there is a certain focus on Europe on Europe, especially Italy.
"It will be interesting to see whether the new pope will nominate cardinals from the rest of the world to rebuild the balance," he said.
Forty per cent of Roman Catholics are in South America, but they only have a tiny number of cardinals.
It will also be the first election of cardinals since Francis instituted the Council of Cardinals, a group of eight cardinals from all over the world, tasked with finding ways of reforming the church.
In the past, the pope has ruled everything on his own.
"Now Francis has chosen these eight cardinals to help him," Valero said.
He said it is "quite possible" that these cardinals will be asked by the pope for advice.
But we haven't been in this situation before - it's an absolute novelty.
Valero said the pope has traditionally elevated bishops from big places as cardinals, but Francis is “full surprise – so we don’t know who he appoints.”
GM is withdrawing some new U.S.-to-sleeping of seat backrests
According to Friday's announcement from the U.S. automotive safety authority, General Motors is withdrawing nearly 19,000 new (2014-)-sleequilibrated Chevrolet Silverado and GMC Sierra light trucks to repair the defect with manual tilting of the backrest.
For some of these vehicles, the front-seater tilt mechanism may have a glitch.
As a result, seat backrests do not comply with federal automotive safety standards for head restraints.
Announcables placed on the website of the State Traffic Confidentiality Administration (NHTSA) say that "if the car gets hit from the back, the head restraint does not have to properly protect passengers, which increases the risk of an injury."
The downloaded models were made between August 1 and September 10.
GM launched the shipment in June, and for the leading U.S. automaker, this is the most important launch of the vehicle from restructure to bankruptcy in 2009.
GM informed the owner of the van about a glitch in the first mid-October.
NHTSA was unable to re-examine an announcement to owners due to the government's 16-Daily malfunction, which weakened growth in car sales in October.
Securities from Silverado and Sierra deliveries that were redesigned for 2014 have risen to about 20 per cent over the first 10 months of this year, GM said on Friday.
In October, GM sold 42,660 Silverado and 16, 503 Sierra models.
Shares on the New York Stock Exchange rose 1.4 percent to $37.47 on Friday afternoon.
Desplied shrullen shriek by the Obama elector
I'm voting for Obama twice - I've shared the hope of a possible change
Says Obama's Honest Educated Obstructions of the Republican Party
Obstructive policy cannot excuse the woes of Obama's health care websites or unmanned aeronautical attacks
"Obam's 2008 Obama campaign memo is a sad commemoration of what could have happened
Nathaniel P. Morris has been reheviding medicine at Harvard.
I'm reading a very sad book these days.
About this book I thought it would cheer me up during the sloccuquious days of my second-year medical school and rebuild an ounce of hope.
It's called "The Audacity to Win", and it's memories of Barack Obama's 2008 presidential campaign.
When I finish patient testimonials in the evening and go to bed, the book brings me back to a time when politics inspired millions of people and the speeches took your breath away.
Elections ended in overwhelming predominance, and TV news forced us to reflect on the historical value of the moment.
My classmates wept with joy, and my parents hid the newspaper they'd found.
A young team of visionaries went to the White House and the nation was ready for a change.
During his inauguration in 2008, Obama held support of 82 percent.
And I'm closing the book here.
Going to the present is a harsh awakening, like waking up from a dream.
It's hard to remember these optimistic days - they seem to be distant remembrance, a sad reminder of wasted opportunities.
In the years after throwing in my first ballot, there have indeed been changes.
It certainly wasn't any of what I was imagining.
Acknowledging that Obama has achieved a number of great and diverse achievements – from the law of affordable care through the withdrawal of our troops from Iraq and the end of politics “not to ask, say nothing” after the killing of Osama bin Ládin.
In addition, I am convinced that party obstructions have reversed so many attempts to move our nation forward: among others, it is an imigration reform, the possibility for the public to choose health care and the closure of a base in Guantnám.
But after a lot of situations where I was defending the Obama administration in front of colleagues and comrades, I've reached an absolute limit when it comes to an explanation.
I found myself in a place of political hopelessness.
Obstructions of Republicans can not explain the approval of eavesdropping on foreign leaders or air raids of unmanned aeronautical jet that kill innocent children overseas.
It cannot be explained that the National Security Agency collects data on the private lives of the Americans, nor that it persecutes those who uncover the wrong actions of the government.
They can't be held accountable for the execution of Anwar al-Awlaki, an U.S. citizen, without a proper trial or for curtailing public donations and spending during presidential campaigns.
It is not possible to justify the findings of a report, which states that the White House's efforts to silence the media are "the most aggressive ... from the Nixon administration."
And most recently, they cannot excuse the incapacity to create more than 3 years after the signing of an Affordable Care, a simple website.
I don’t even know if something like this could be expected.
At the age of 18, I should have guessed that government power could be inconsistent with the political campaigns that had preceded it.
Election is clearly unpredictable and our public talks will be shaped by an opposing political party and arrogant events such as the Newton massacre.
However, all the examples I have mentioned above seem to a large extent to be the outcome of the administration's own decision.
And that bothers me the hardest.
I chose Obama again in 2012, but not because I was enthusiastic about his candidacy.
Mitt Romney was a grizzling and unbalanced alternative, and it didn’t seem that he couldn’t cement his policies or position.
I had the feeling that the second term of office for Obama, liberated from the pressure of the next election, would fulfill the hopes that we once had heard of.
Nevertheless, a return to 2008 – as support for Obama falls below 45% this week – more and harder through this book.
It makes me think of a number of vulnerabilities that have faded.
This week I read in the book a chapter on how Obama suffered an outrageous defeat to Clinton during the primaries in Pennsylvania.
At an additional meeting during the campaign, he told his staff that he had to get back to the pace and remain loyal to his mission.
"I want our magic back," he said.
Let's not forget who we are.
It's been five years, Mr. President, and I totally agree.
Thoughts expressed in this rescheduling are exclusively Nathaniel Morris.
