RBS Suspenses Two Foreign Currency Dealers
According to two people familiar with the situation, the Scottish Royal Bank (RBS) is suspending two traders in its foreign-currency division, another suggestion that a global investigation by supervisory authorities on suspected currency market machinations is becoming increasingly common.
Some the world's largest banks, including UBS, Barclays, Deutsche Bank and RBS, have confirmed they are collaborating with competent authorities to investigate the world's largest-growing financial market, where they change owners of $5.3 trillion every day.
The mentioned two traders would be the first RBS employees suspended as part of an expanding investigation, which is a response to the LIBOR manipulation scandal (londe inter-bank interest rate).
The bank declined to comment on the suspension, but confirmed that it had received a request for information from the authorities this month.
“Our relentless quisers on this issue continues and we are fully collaborating with FCA and our other authorities,” the bank said.
Last month, people who are close to the event reported that RBS had handed over to the British financial conduct authority records of emails and instant messages sent from the accounts and the accounts of the former trader.
This trader, Richard Usher, abandoned RBS in 2010 and is believed to have been released from his current position as the European foreign currency trading president at JPMorgan.
Rohan Ramchandani, the president-equiry for ready-money trading at Citi, went on holiday this week, while Matt Gardiner, a former head of currency trading in Barclays and UBS, was suspended by Standard Chartered this week.
None of these merchants have been charged with a misdemeanor.
Mr Usher's instant messaging group included bankers from Barclays and Citigroup, people initiations said.
UBS said earlier this week that it had made action against any of its employees after the Swiss control body of Finma announced that it had been investigating suspicious machinations of a foreign currency market in several Swiss banks, it had taken action against some of its employees.
Worldwide, at least six authorities - the European Commission, Finma, Swiss anti-monopoly Weko, FCA, the U.S. Department of Attorney and the Hong Kong Monetary Authority - is looking into claiming that these bankers have conspired to move the currency market.
In addition, HSBC, Citigroup, JPMorgan and Credit Suisse launched an in-internal investigation by HSBC or received a request for information from the competent authorities.
The banks combed years-old quick messages and e-mails in order to find cases of misdemeanors.
Investigation reports have alarmed retailers, which was one of the world's largest profit-making engines in investment bank shutdowns in past years, but which has been disrupted by the fact that the low instability of currencies has curtailed opportunities for speculators.
Some bankers tried to reduce the gravity when they argued that it is basically impossible to manipulate a large and highly liquid market with foreign currency, but high-ranking traders say that this is not necessarily true.
A high-profile trader has said that, despite the significant volume of daily trading in foreign currency, liquidity-execution between different trading platforms and banks increasing the use of their own internal platforms, it means “you can start having an effect on the market at relatively low prices.”
The news came on the previous day Credit Suisse announced it had released this week from its share trading department, which at the end of last year caused a loss of nearly $6 million.
The bank immediately alerted the relevant authorities and cooperates with their control authorities.
"We are convinced that this trader acted alone and that this problem has been resolved," Credit Suisse said.
