Supreme Court Supports Obama's Healthcare Bill
U.S. Supreme Judiciary has ruled that a health care law signed by Barack Obama is in line with the constitution, a crucial victory for the Obama administration.
With a vote of 5:4, the court has ruled that an individual regulation of the Patient Protection Act and Affordable care – ordering citizens to purchase health insurance by 2014, otherwise they are at risk of paying a fine – it does not contradict the constitution on the basis of a government’s tax authority.
Court President John Roberts stood on the side of four more liberal members of the court, while judges Scalia, Thomas, Alito and Kennedy were in opposition.
The court also endorsed the remaining parts of the 2,700-page law, as well as that the legal requirement for health care, which requires states to expand the possibility of acquiring sickness insurance, otherwise there is a risk of deprivation of all the financing of sickness insurance from federal sources, is not coercivious and inconsistent with the constitution.
A lawsuit was filed in order to block this law by 26 states and the National Federation of Independent Enterprises.
