Egypt launches the first Free-Velebrated President
Mohammed Morsi took an inaugural oath in an inaugural sanctuary, but his triumphal day will hardly end political unrest in Egypt.
ISLAMISTA Mohammed Morsi pledged “new Egypt” when he was solemnly inaugurated as the first free-equity president of this country, after Hosní Mubarak, who was dethroned 16 months earlier.
During his inauguration before the Supreme Constitutional Court, Morsi also became the first freely-elected Islamic president in an Arab world and the Fifth-Egypt head of state since the overthrow of the monarchy about 60 years ago.
He sworn an ossument before 18 judges dressed in black robes in a mansion built with a view to the Nile, which is reminisnced by an ancient Egyptian temple.
"We strive for a better tomorrow, a new Egypt and the second republic," Morsi said at a ceremonial ceremonial broadcast live on state television.
“Egyptian people today laid the foundation of a new life—absolute liberties, true democracies and steadicia,” Mursi, a 60-year-old engineer graduated in the U.S. and a Muslim Brother-Smeltiac, a fundamentalist group that has been the target of sharp attacks by individual governments for most 84 years since its inception as an illicit organization.
When Morsi arrived in a small column of automobiles shortly after 11 a.m. local p.m., the building was guarded by hundreds of soldiers and police officers.
Just a few hundred supporters gathered in front of the court building to celebrate the presidency, and unlike Mubarah's presidential pomp, traffic was only briefly halted, so that a motorcade could pass a usually bustlinder connecting the city's center with suburbs in the souvenirs in the south.
Mursi's personal prestige, ridiculably referred to as an uncharismatic "responsive tire", has risen sharply since his victory and his speech on Friday, which aimed to present it not only as a candidate of the Islamists, but also to all those who want to bring the rebellion towards the authoritarian Mubarak.
"Egypt today is a civil, national, constitutional and modern state," Morsi, dressed in a blue formal suit with a red tie to judges-panelled house-panelled sworn.
Morsi later traveled to the University of Cairo to give an inaugural speech.
While he stood at attention, he received an official welcome from a military band that played the national anthem.
There was a military commander of field marshal Hussein Tantawi.
His Arrival was accompanied by the chant “Armide and the people are one hand” from the mouths of hundreds of people assembled in the main lecture hall of the university.
The University of Cairo was founded in 1908 as a secular education center and later in the 70's became a bastion for Islamic student movements.
Mursi swore a symbolic oath in Tahrir Square on Friday, the place where a revolution was resumed, which last year ended Mubarak's authoritarian government and solemonially vowed to re-separize the presidential powers removed from his office by a military adviser that took them from the expulsed leader.
But by agreeing to pass an official oath before the court, rather than before the parliament, as is customary, he acquiesied the will of the army, suggesting that the struggle for power will continue.
Morsi's speech on Tahrir was full of dramatic populist gestures.
In the ghettos there will probably be police specialists, to alleviate tensions
Police specialists should work in ghettos in the Czech Republic in the future.
They'd spend up to half their errands here.
They would keep an eye on the order and solve the problems.
Thanks to their knowledge of the environment, they should contribute to the reduction of tensions between people from poor places and residents from the surrounding area.
The Ministry of the Interior is counting on this in its proposal to reduce security risks.
According to him, "accelerated provision of social housing" would mainly help to improve the situation in the country.
The document was debated by the government.
according to the analysis, there were about 300 slums and neighborhoods in the Czech Republic a few years ago.
There were 8,000 people, especially Romani people.
Entrepreneurs in ghettos were out of work, families depended on benefits, the children ended up in "strange" schools.
There's no weave.
In the meantime, according to experts of problem localities, there have been a hundred.
The relationships between their inhabitants and others are escalating.
Anti-Romani marches took place in several places.
Extremists were joined by people from the surrounding area.
According to the interior, it tends to be difficult for the police in areas with ghettos to maintain security and order.
An essential condition is "consistent application of equal access in law enforcement".
However, it is not possible to achieve this without a detailed knowledge of the place and people.
A specialist would go errands in the ghetto, where he would spend up to half of his service, the rest would serve in his district.
In a troubled locality he would oversuocompete the order.
He would also know the locals, so he could better deal with problems or offenses.
"Dautches of established confidence, specialists will be more easily able to prevent a number of wrongdoings," he said.
Specialists in the ghettos would receive surcharges, up to 1000 crowns a month.
Introducing special police officers into problem areas is to be covered by Norwegian funds.
The budget is about 600,000 euros (approximately 15.5 million kroner now).
The Czech Republic pays 15 per cent of the sum, funds 85 per cent.
Preparation began this year, now a detailed suggestion is being made.
The project is expected to last until 2016.
The authors took inspiration from the neighbors.
This mechanism has been successfully applied for several years in Slovakia, where there is an incomparably higher problem with socially explicated localities than in the Czech Republic.
"There are so-called Romani settlements here," the authors of the report said.
How many specialists there should be in the Czech Republic is not yet clear.
The answer should be given by an analysis, which will determine the places where a special policeman would be needed.
It will also depend on how much money the police will be able to vaccinate every year after the end of the project.
According to the project's proposal, selected police officers first undergo an intern internship in Slovakia.
This training should be carried out every year by five dozen members.
Of them, the most suitable candidates will emerge.
There should also be a special education for police officers who will work in the ghettos.
According to the authors, mainly social housing would help to improve the situation.
It should motivate and lead to integration.
According to the ideas of the interior, there would have three categories.
The minimum standard would meet hostels with precisely defined rules, such as the number of people per room.
A medium-sized standard would be in smaller apartments.
A higher standard would have social apartments, which would be comparable to conventional housing.
Negotiators would have to carry out an integration plan to be drawn up by social workers.
They would have to obey the accommodation rules, take care of their health, send children to schools, re-qualify themselves and actively look for a job.
If they were striving for incorporation, they would proceed to a higher housing standard.
According to the interior, the permeable housing system should work for at least 30 years, until the next generation "in a pro-integration-motivated environment".
Social Housing models are being prepared by ministries for local development and work.
Propose it until the end of the year.
Karel Drábek: Uhlířská vrch - one of our youngest volcanoes.
Uhlířské vrch is a conspicuously hill 3 km southeast of Bruntál.
Along with a few others it is evidence of volcanic activity in Nizký Jeseník at a time (from a geological point of view).
The last eruptions were still there at the beginning of the Quaternary.
When viewed from a greater distance, there is a conspicuous white-plated Baroque pilgrimage Church of Our Lady of the Auxiliary, built between 1755 - 1765.
Replacing an original wooden pilgrimage chapel.
The inn, which was standing next to the servitude, did not survive.
Shortly after the completion of the church an alley was planted from four rows of linden, about 1 km long.
The cross road on Uhlířské vrch is already the third version, the original here stood after 1916, the second, from 2001 it lasted only until 2004.
Fifteen stops are wooden sculptures, which were carried out by local artistic carver František Nedomlel.
It has used various types of trees from the Low Jeseník region.
Equally, stalls from rocks from the local area form an interesting geological exposition.
At each stop there is also the name of the sponsor.
Uhlířské vrch is a remnant of a young volcano.
Although the data from radiometric measurements of old age vaguely vague varies, the last eruption here was about 1.5 million years ago.
It is therefore younger than the Great Roudný (yelving 3.3 million years of age) and older than Venus's volcano (yet 0.8 million years old).
The volcano originated by an explosion.
During it she threw out a considerable amount of volcanic ashes, along with fragments of rock from the subsoil, which by firing obtained a reddish-brown color.
At the end of this phase, lava poured into the surface - today it is a layer of porous basalt up to 13 meters powerful.
The second eruption eavesped red, brown-and-nangle and in some places even black tuff.
Their layer is powerful for up to 50 meters and its part can be accessed in the refraction wall.
There are conspicuous volcano bombs of up to 1 a metre. The second eruption was terminated by the outbursement of lava.
At the end of the activity from the volcano poured a lava, which today consists of up to 50 m a powerful layer of sausceptible basalt.
volcanoes that alternating volcanic accelerating volcanic accelerations with lava flows are the most common types of volcanoes.
Because they're actually composed of layers, they've been named stratvolcan.
The original volcano cone was subordinatelously succumbing to erosion.
However, he was endangered by the mining of rocks in a quarry on the southern side.
The most intensive logging took place here between 1953 and 1962.
Tufs were added to the concrete, which was then lighter and had better thermal insulating properties.
Mining was stopped under the pressure of experts and the public, thus preserving this unique place.
However, the problem lies in the porosity and little coherence of the rocks, which quickly weather, crumble and slide down the slope.
The quarry is disappearing relatively quickly.
In order to make things even more complicate, a warm, south-facing slope is an ideal place for some rare species of plants and animals.
Remeasure is therefore problematic, but over time it will be necessary to select and maintain a characteristic geological profile.
It is nothing new, similar profiles are maintained in a number of important locations.
Uhlířský vrch can be reached after the green tourist sign from Bruntál, or from Silesian Kočov.
Automobilists can use an unpaid parking lot at the beginning of a liquor alley, where even a short educational trail begins.
From the villa she moved into a prefabricated building.
Ivana grew up and spent a greater part of her life in an apartment villa with a garden and a swimming pool in one of Prague's most beautiful residential neighborhoods in Ořechovka.
However, the original owners of the house regained it in resituation and the tenants had to move away with a small severity.
That’s why Ivana quickly searched for alternative housing for herself and her already older mum.
From the acquaintances she received a tip for an apartment 3 + kk in an older panel house on the opposite side of Prague.
"At first it was a shock, but the view from the fourth floor on a large forest park and the dam impregnated me on the other side," recalls Ivana at the first impression.
At first I thought it would be a make-up for about three years.
"Today we have been living here for more than thirteen years, and not only because of the gradual refurbishment, I wouldn't change it," the owner adds.
At that time my mother and her daughter were moving from a one-hundred-timete apartment to a smaller one with an area of 62 m2 and handed out almost all the equipment.
The action wasn't easy, things were stored with friends, and it lasted almost a year.
The new apartment was in quite good condition, but they had to take the necessary modifications, such as varnishing doors or replacing the windows.
The previous owner demolished the nucleus, but the owner was most annoyed by an impractically designed and unnecessarily large hallway, and vice versa a small bathroom.
Combining two rooms created a multifunctional living space.
The architects used plasterboard to demarcate the individual zones and the structure of the room, which allowed for a new placement of the lights.
The first intervention, which after a couple of years decided, was the removal of the rung in the living room.
In this way, the apartment changed the disposition to 2 + kk, but the room gained airiness and opened a panoramic view of the surrounding greenery.
The next step to the partial improvement of the standard was to replace the PVC floor with laminate.
Ivana didn’t know what to do with the bathroom.
I've been taking home magazines for years looking for inspiration.
I recently discovered an interesting realization in one of them.
On the recommendation I approached young architect Kamila Douděrová.
"It's actually the most important step in the future," Ivana said.
Thanks to the redevelopment of the bathroom, it was also possible to adjust the dispensing of the hallway at the same time.
The architect connected the space of the toilet and the bathroom and replaced the two doors with the opening wings with some sliding doors, thus saving space.
The bathroom is time-temporary and also barrier-free enough.
High-quality compresses imitate sandstone, there is also a comfortable seal in the shower, a bidet shower at the toilet.
Successful cooperation with Kamila Douděrová continued.
As part of the program How to build a dream took place a quick but fundamental transformation of the living room, incorporation in which architect Martin Čeněk also participated.
We had to get three functions into the space, which originated by combining two smaller rooms, dining, residential and sleeping, without helping ourselves with the classical principles of optical cutting, for example semi-trips or Japanese walls.
"In the result, it would violate the openness and unequivment of the space," explains the architect.
It was originally possible to detached the sleeping-feathing and the living area.
It was enough to turn two sofas back to each other, and between them to build a bespoke made cabinet for duvets.
The dining area is demarcated by the shades of the floor and painting.
The architect used a high-quality marmoleum on the floor in the kitchen and the dining area, she revived the sliding doors with a pattern of wallpaper.
If necessary, the kitchen corner is covered by sliding panels with wallpaper.
The new floor made of anti-allergic marmolea has a number of advantages compared to the previous laminate: a softer non-slip surface without joints and better sound insulation.
The system of niches and glaider ceilings highlighted by paint is an example of how it is possible to work with space in a prefabricated building.
I left the colors to the architects, I wanted it to be joyful.
"I like orange, I had the same shade in the previous apartment," says Ivana.
Grafoil compresses with blooming magnolia motif is practical and creates a distinctive aesthetic element.
For a warm energetic tone, the architects chose a white one that underlined the modern functional style.
In a small apartment it was possible to store books, among other things, in the space under the windowsill.
Everything is within reach, the lamps are conveniently operated remotely.
In the redesigned interior there is an excellent contra contrast of the new with an elder, for example with a decades-and--year-old crystal chandelier even with the high-quality art objects that Ivana has been collecting all her life.
Latary was the kitchen.
Cabinets used to be positioned on both sides and the owner felt a little bit like in a tunnel.
The architect deprived their number and rearranged the layout.
Furnishing in a simple design is made to order, the white area is softened by a slightly sepheledic surface.
Thanks to an imaginative solution, a washing machine, a tumble dryer and a washer.
Appearance of a small kitchen revived an all-glass cladding of the walls above the workbench.
A unique graph glass with a digital-printed theme is attractive, moreover, it is also excellently maintained.
Ivana chose magnolia blooms reminiscent of the favorite neighborhood in which she lived for years.
Now she seems to be happy here, too.
In the original bathroom there was a small bathtub and a washbasin, for the new owner she chose a neutral shade of tiles.
A large shower has a Ronal Glass Door.
The task was to create from a given environment a clear simple space, in which functions and devices will be in harmony.
We unified the interior materially and colorfully and attempted its sleek, cosy, not overcrowded appearance.
"Another condition was a barrier-free solution with a minimum of hurdles and the possibility of easy movement around the furniture and achievability of everyday objects," adds Kamila Douděrová.
The Supreme Court has ruled: Nečas can be prosecuted.
The Supreme Court has ruled that former Prime Minister Petr Nečas can also be prosecuted for any criminal offences that he could have committed before the August dissolution of the Chamber of Deputies.
It was protected only by procedural immunity, which refers to manifestations in the Chamber of Deputies.
In the case of former ODS deputies Ivan Fuksa, Mark Šnajdr and Petr Tluchoř, on the contrary, he ruled that they cannot be punished for any criminal offences until they renounced their parliamentary mandate last November, Supreme Court spokesman Petr Knötig said.
With this decision, the court dismissed the proposal of the Public Prosecutor Ivo Ištvan, who wanted to continue the criminal proceedings with the deputies.
According to him, they accepted a bribe of lucrative posts in state-owned companies for waivering a mandate and will not cause problems when passing laws.
On the contrary, it is possible to prosecute the former prime minister, who offered them these posts.
