Primordial Mayor Kajnar: I Am Not Cancer in ČSSD
Before the e-election, a long-summered controversy between two flanks fighting for influence within the Ostrava ČSSD was fully flared.
Everything culminated when the mayor of Ostrava Petr Kajnar went to the court for undemocratic compilation of candidates.
Nevertheless, they did not want to comment too much on this, so as not to harm the party even more before the elections.
Voters' votes are now counted.
Immediately on Monday evening, the leadership of the Ostrava ČSSD urged the mayor of Ostrava to get out of the party, and it will probably also be pressed to lose his position.
Petr Kajnar still wants to fight.
What the disputes are about and who is on whose side, he described in an exclusive interview for Deník.
How do you view the outcome of the election?
CSSD certainly counted on a much better result.
Has she been harmed by the fact that disputes within the party were dealt with in the media before the elections?
I can imagine that the Social-Democracy could have a better electoral outcome.
But on the other hand, it corresponds to the state in which the ČSSD is now.
It's a party that has its internal problems, it's intrinsically ill, and people can feel that everything is not right in it.
They made it clear in the election.
What Are the Internal Illances of the CSSD?
According to ODS has undergone bankruptcy for quite some time, and such things have unfortunately taken place in social democracy.
Lobbyists, influential people who had previously expected to find their prebends from the ODS, have moved towards the people of the ČSSD and have an influence on them.
Then it is related to the fact that the offices of the Ostrava municipality go police and collect computers.
It shows that the ČSSD isn't composed only of people who would like the good of everyone else, but many of them think primarily of their own.
CSSD is divided into two irreconcilable camps even in Prague, as can be seen even in the current struggle for power between Haškovs and Sobotkovs.
But even this has been prepared for a long time.
What happened before the elections in Ostrava was just a foreshivulation of what is happening now in Prague.
Haška's wing was gradually occupying the positions so that after the elections it could provoke a situation weakened by Sobotka's flank.
One is in politics in a situation where you sometimes have to come to compromise.
However, everyone has an intrinsic boundary, when they can no longer make those compromises, and this is also my case.
Originally I did not want to run for the Chamber of Deputies, but when I perceived the whole thing inside the party, I agreed with Luboš Zaorálek that I would try to get into the regional candidate to strengthen Sobotka's wing as mayor of Ostrava.
Hašek's wing, however, gave it a stop.
Why are you on the side of Bohuslav Sobotka, not Michal Hašek?
Slávek Sobotka, in my opinion, is a person who always talks primarily about the ČSSD program and, to a certain extent, about visions for the party and for this country.
Admittedly, I haven't heard anything like that on the other.
Michal Hašek just fights for power.
I don’t understand how the bureau, which is a lower-minded institution than the Central Executive Committee, can only start dismissing the party’s chairman.
This is a struggle for power, regardless of everything and against the statutes of the party.
According to me, what Michal Hašek has now demonstrated in Prague ended his political career.
How did you perceive the fact that the Ostrava leadership of the ČSSD did not applaute you for a candidate.
Political parties are based on the competition, who will be among their members.
For the most part, the competition should largely be based on programmatic visions and the abilities of those people to fulfill those visions.
Of course, people who don’t even have a lot of abilities or visions apply for this competition, but a political party is an elevator to money they wouldn’t get anywhere else.
The problem is when these people start to come to the fore, thanks to the fact that they are violating the rules without scruples, violating the statutes of the party.
By their practices they make the mafia from the political parties and, in my opinion, they also violate the laws of the Czech Republic.
Drawing attention to this problem is more important to me than taking into account whether a Kajnar has a political future.
It is imperative that the parties begin to respect the statutes.
That’s why you filed an application before the elections before the elections for a preliminary injunction against the ČSSD candidate in the Moravian-Silesian region?
The current district executive committee of the ČSSD in Ostrava strengthens its power by the fact that organizations that do not suit it can cancel, purposefully establish new organizations, gradually throwing out inconvenient members of the party.
It is also an example that they wanted to expel Richard Bednařík from the club of representatives, but he filed a lawsuit against it, and then the resolution on his executability was canceled.
This demonstrates the fact that to seek compliance with the party's statutes through the courts is the last option, but at the moment when those other ways and control bodies within the ČSSD have ceased to work, it is the only option.
Efquation of the leadership of the Ostrava ČSSD headed by MP Adam Rykála is to remove you as mayor.
It is an open secret that the mayor of Poruba Lumír Palyza has a glimp on this post.
That's right, Adam Rykala and Lumír Palyza are trying to do their best to call me off.
Promise people's positions, they get votes, and for those votes they strengthen their power in the party.
When I go back to the past, everything that may have taken place around Baník Ostrava and the purchase of the Bazaly stadium was just a prerogative for them so that they could attack me.
It was artificially staged so that they could call me a person who puts the money where it doesn’t belong.
At the same time, the city has bought property, which is strategically very valuable for it.
So, how do you see it further with your staying as mayor?
The district committee already urged me and other people on Monday to consider staying in the ČSSD.
But I don't think it's like a burglar screaming: Grab a thieves!
When we look at the results of my work over the last eleven years, I don’t see anyone in Ostrava’s social-democracy to initiate and assert, sometimes against party members, the emergence of so many developmental things, such as industrial zones or the expansion of a science-technology park.
Yet everything becomes inconvenient.
I don’t find it any other way than to hinder the others to go into the places they desire.
Are you still going to fight against these efforts?
Because if this republic is supposed to work well, political parties also have to work well.
It is hard to believe that any party will be able to effectively run the state if it can’t even control itself.
So I'm not contemplating that I'm going away from the ČSSD right now because I don't think anyone who's causing this party's cancer is not me, but these people who are trying to piss me off.
