Six out-of-to-ten companies are being prepared for the new Civil Code
As of January 1, 2014, six companies out of ten are intensively preparing for the imminent effectiveness of the new Civil Code and the Business Corporations Act.
Just eight percent of the representatives surveyed said they didn't deal with the issues related to both key codes in their company at all.
This is according to a survey by Ambruz & Dark Deloitte Legal, which surveyed the opinions of 108 Czech companies.
In connection with the imminent deadline for the effectiveness of the two key codes at the last minute, a number of managers are starting to be aware of the significant impact on the business of their companies.
The biggest changes in private law in the last 50 years will, in our opinion, affect all citizens and companies in the Czech Republic.
Due to the pollination of politicians, the business sector has long lived in the uncertainty whether the revolutionary changes will eventually take place or not.
"But now they only have a number of weeks left in order for the transition to a new right take place without complications for their companies," Jan Spáčil's Ambruz & Dark Deloitte partner commented.
Of the 61 perceivable companies intensively engaged in the new laws, 26 percent said their companies had already taken all the necessary measures to be prepared in time.
Furthermore, 35 per cent of these companies are preparing to take the necessary measures in the near future.
Against this, every fifth company has not dealt with recodifications, yet they have a concrete idea of how to prepare for them.
A total of 11 per cent do not have an opinion and are unable to assess the issue.
Only eight per cent of companies admit they haven't dealt with recodencies so far.
The most important legal theme associated with the new Civil Code is for the largest number of subscribed companies to conclude new contracts (21 per cent), followed by the solution of business conditions (16 percent) as well as changes in the cadastre of real estate and liability for damage (after ten percent).
For the treaties there will be one of the most important innovations of the entire recodification, namely the unification of the regulation of contracts only within the framework of the new Civil Code.
The simultaneous parallel regulation in the Civil and Commercial Code expires, as a result of which the contractual law should become clearer for citizens and entrepreneurs.
"In the case of entrepreneurs, the new regulation will offer a lot of novelties with an emphasis on the greater freedom of the arrangement of relationships," said Ambruz & Dark Deloitte Legal attorney Lukáš Poddaný.
In connection with the Business Corporations Act, almost a quarter of companies consider the issue of increasing the liability of statutory bodies to be the most important topic.
Furthermore, there are topics related to changes in the convening and progress of the general meeting (15 per cent) and corporate representation (14 per cent).
The new legislation constitutes the tightening of obligations and possible impacts for statutory bodies, especially in a situation where a company is threatened with bankruptcy.
"In practice, this means that if the members of the board of directors and the executives of the companies do not act with the care of a proper manager and do not avert an imminent bankruptcy of the company, they can lose the benefit gained for the performance of the office only in the last two years," Spáčil added.
