It travels through mud, the river and the jungle and provides free medical care
Dr. Georges Bwelle provides free medical care in Kameruun villages.
Bwelle and his team treat hundreds of patients almost every weekend
There are not too many doctors in the countries of West Africa; there is only one per 5,000 inhabitants.
Vote here or via your mobile phone.
Dr. Georges Bwelle is one of the top 10 CNN candidates for CNN Heroes of the Year 2013.
You’ll be able to hold a vote for him or any of the ten main candidates in the CNN Heroes of the Year 2013 competition.
The winner will receive $250,000 so that he can continue his extraordinary work.
For 21 years Georges Bwelle watched his father fall into unconsciousness again and again and go to hospitals that were unequivoied to help.
Jamef Bwelle was injured in a car accident near Cameroonian capital Yaounde in 1981.
At first he was healed only with a fractured arm, but he subsequently developed an infection that had spread to the brain, creating a hematoma that gave him problems all his life.
“ There were no neurosurgeons in Cameroon,” says Georges Bwell.
We'd have to take him outside Cameroon if we had the money.
Instead, Bwelle has spent years transporting his father into overcrowded clinics and hospitals, where he was given a treatment that had simply been available.
"It is not easy," Bwelle says.
You can leave the house at five o'clock in the a.m., hurry to the hospital so that you'll be there first, you're not.
There are a lot of patients.
Some people only die because they have to wait.
The situation hasn't changed much since Bwellee's father died in 2002.
According to the World Medical Organization, there is only one doctor per 5,000 inhabitants in Cameroon.
Just for comparison, the ratio in the United States is one doctor for 413 inhabitants.
And even if Kamerunec doctors were to be able to visit, many wouldn't be able to afford it.
Two out of five people in this country live below the poverty line, and nearly three-quarters of health care spending in the country is private.
"The only problem they have is poverty," Bwelle says.
For the sake of poverty, they can't enjoy their lives.
When Bwelle saw his father and many of his compatriots suffer, he decided to do something about it.
Dr. Georges Bwelle and his team of volunteers performed a free of 700 surgeries last year.
He became a vascular vascular steeder doctor at Yaounde.
And he founded a non-non-non-non-non-non-non-provincial organization ASCOVIME, whose members visit rural areas over the weekend, where they provide free medical care.
Since 2008, he has been assisting nearly 32,000 people with his group of volunteers.
Almost every Friday he and up to 30 people are packed into the vacancies, attach medical supplies to the roofs and travel through the rugged terrain to villages that need help.
They don't always be accompanied by happiness.
More than once, vehicles have to push through the river and mud.
But when it arrives, they are greeted as true heroes: a fiancé, singing and dancing, and the best accommodation that the community can offer.
In these villages, free medical care really is the reason to celebrate and Bwele – with its broad smile and unbridled energy – is very willing to engage in local merriment.
The next day, the team starts treating hundreds of patients.
"We've got 500 patients on every quest," Bwelle says.
They come to the village from a radius of 60 kilometers on foot.
During each of these weekends, doctors provide various medical assistance.
Many are treated with malaria, tuberculosis, malnutrition, diabetes, parasitic diseases and sexually transmitted diseases.
Others can get crutches or donated goggles for free – a document demanded by a school, but which many miserable Familiar Families simply can’t afford.
In the evening, the team performs simple surgeries under local anaesity.
Surgerys are usually done in a school building, a town hall or at home; after the operation, the patient wakes up and goes to the recovery site to make room for another patient.
Using light from the generator, the operating room and sanitary equipment Bwelle and its volunteers work until the early hours of the morning.
It's frustrating-intensulate, but village musicians usually help the team not to lose motivation.
"In order to stay awake and continue to work, they've been playing drums all the night," Bwelle says.
On Sunday the team will head back into the city, weary, but proud of their work.
This group - consisting of Cameroonian doctors and foreign medical students - carried out 700 free surgeries last year and realizes that their help can mean a lot for local residents.
A man has said he'll be able to work again after a hernia operation.
“This will change the future for our family,” the man said.
In addition to organizing these weekend clinics and the work of a Surgeon at Bwelle Hospital, he also works at night for private medical clinics around Yaounde.
He says that this second thesis finances about 60 % of its non-non-profit activities; the remainder is covered by private contributions.
“I’m not sure she’s actually sleeping,” says Katie O'Malley, who has been practicing medicine at Drexel’s University in Philadelphia for the past few years.
He's always either in a hospital or he's trying to raise money for an organization so he can do these campaigns.
For medical and nursing students such as O'Malley, who came from the U.S. or Europe to join Bwelle on his mission, it's a great practice they wouldn't get at home.
"We were wiiding our hands thoroughly for Dr. Bwell and removing blood stains or holding his tools," O'Malley said.
You’ll never experience that as a second-grade medical learner in America.
Voluntale students usually pay their way to Cameroon on their own and often arrive with donated medical supplies.
But as soon as they come to Yaounde, food, transport and study are provided by Bwell.
"Undoubtedly he's a hero," O'Malley says.
This organization puts his life and its desire to help the inhabitants of Cameroon is endless.
For Bwele's constant harness is not a burden.
Enamel to help others live more happily, fulfilling the vow he made to his father is something that brings him great joy.
"I'm so happy when I'm doing this job," Bwelle says.
And I'm thinking about my father.
I hope he sees what I'm doing.
To make people laugh, to alleviate their pain - that's why I'm doing this.
Take a look at the ASCOVIME website and you’ll find how you can help.
Minister Holcát reinstated the function of the Chief Sister for the Czech Republic
The head nurse for the Czech Republic should represent the interests of nurses whose number in the Czech Republic exceeds 100 thousand.
"I consider it essential that this function be clearly defined and formalized, so that nurses have a representative within the structure of the office - a representative to which they can always contact if necessary," Martin Holcát, the Minister of Health, explained.
I cherish the work of the nurses and I think that the field of nursing deserves to be given due attention from the ministry.
In the exercise of this vocation requires not only high professional professionalism, but also a human approach and personal conviction.
“I will gladly and with all responsibility try to advance those steps that can improve the position of nurses.”
"We have excellent and skilful nurses in our health care, but I would also like to appeal to those colleagues who, by their approach to the patients, do not help us to exercise their calls with all empathy and to help us improve the image of nurses in the eyes of the public," says Alena Šmídová.
Alena Šmídová’s newly appointed Head Nurse for the Czech Republic on the occasion of her inauguration has also stated that she wants to deal with various aspects of a nurse’s position and intends to cooperate with professional associations, professional societies, educators, other non-medical vocations and doctors.
"Basically I do not want to separate the profession of doctors and nurses, but on the contrary, I see them standing next to each other, everyone has their competencies and, together with other healthcare professionals, form a well-coordinate work team that provides health services for the benefit of the patient."
"I myself encounter a number of problems that occur in the exercise of the profession of nurses, I perceive these difficulties and I intend to participate in their solution."
These include, for example, ensuring the simplification of Nursing and medical records.
"From my own explanatory experience, I know that some administrative tasks are unnecessitantly burdened by nurses and take away the time they could and should rather dedicate to patients," says Alena Šmídová.
Other areas it intends to focus on includes the improvement of working conditions, finding ways for better cooperation in the team and with the management of health service providers, adjustment of competencies, as well as employment issues, such as compliance with the Labor Code or non-complaination of nurses with their insufficient numbers at a given time and place.
We often hear anonymous homesickness, which, out of fear of possible retribution, are not documented in a specific case where violations of the rules may occur.
"Where the plaintiff is not, there is not even a judge, and so in this case could serve as a guarantee to maintain the anonymity of such a complaint and to have a specific case examined by the competent authorities," concludes Alena Šmídová.
Alena Šmíd graduation graduated from the Secondary Secondary Nursing School of Nurse (graduating in 1978) and the Faculty of Philosophy of the University of Charles University in Prague in the Nursing - Pedagogy study program (graduating in 1992).
Since 2010 he has been the head of the Department of Nursing and other non-medical disciplines and recognizing qualifications at the Department of Education and Science.
Throughout her professional career, she worked as a nurse at a bedside in an in-house department, she also worked as a teacher at the Secondary Medical School, where she was also a statutory deputy headmistress for eight years.
From 2004 to 2009 she worked as the head nurse of the Prison Service of the Czech Republic.
She has published several professional nursing textbooks and articles in professional journals and devoted herself to graduation activities in the field of Nursing.
