Unprecedented confession John Kerry said U.S. espionage "went too far"
John Kerry outlined a refinement of the U.S. defense stance on his surveillance programs and said in an unprecedented confession that in some cases espionage "has gone too far."
The foreign minister also acknowledged that he, along with Barack Obama, is responsible for the fact that, in view of the impedimentsatory revelations about NSA espionage, which emerged thanks to whistleblower Edward Snowden, they acted as an "autopilot".
Published information has sparked a diplomatic thunderstorm between the U.S. government and its allies.
Mr Kerry, via a video-link to an open government conference in London, said: "There is no doubt that both the president and I and the others in the government have indeed learned about some of the things that were happening out of innumerability, because there was the possibility, with reference to the Second World War, a difficult period of the Cold War and, of course, 9/11."
Thus, he became the first high-profile U.S. government who has acknowledged that U.S. espionage has crossed the border, but stressed that it has not been violated anyone's rights.
He said: "In some cases it has gone too far."
And the president is determined to try to explain and clarify this to the people, and he is already conducting a thorough examination so that no one feels that there has been an abolition.
Assume you that there is no abusing the rights of innocent people in this process.
Mr Kerry, however, has insisted that the NSA seeks for good and that its surveillance operations have saved many lives.
“We are dealing with a new world in which people are willing to blow themselves away.”
There is radical extremism in the world, which is firmly committed to murdering and throwing people up and attacking governments.
And why don't you be able to interrepee and stop it before it happens?
In fact, we have prevented planes from crashing, buildings have not exploded, and people have not been executed because we are already able to find out about these plans ahead.
The presiding member of the Senate Subcommorye for European Affairs said on Thursday that U.S. lawmakers were going to Europe to respond to fears of foreign affairs involving alleged U.S. espionage and to convince Europeans the need to continue to join forces in the fight against terrorism.
Senator Chris Murphy of Connecticut said he had spoken this week with members of the European Parliament and others and that he is worried about their threats to stop participating in counter-terrorism organizations because they are dislodged by monitoring the National Security Agency (NSA).
It is indeed important for U.S. national-security interests that Europe stays with us in the same boat, with regard to joint efforts in the fight against Terrorism, Murphy, a member of the Democratic Party and the presiding part-of-security subcommittee on foreign relations on European affairs, he said in an interview.
And I'm going to Europe to make it clear that we have to continue working together against terrorism, despite their rteriot over NSA programs.
News in the media that the NSA collected millions of phone records in Europe violated relations with some U.S. allies, even though the agency chief said this week that they were inaccurately and reflected the misunderstood metadata that NATO allys collected and shared with the United States.
Further revelations quoted Snowden as quoted by Snowden that the NSA had been following German Chancellor Angela Merkel's mobile phone and up to 34 other world leaders.
National Intelligence Director James Clapper has advocated allied espionage as necessary and said it was a normal matter on both sides.
Amid the tumult of Murphy, Murphy said his office was planning a parliamentary trip that should take place this year, and he hopes there will be members of both parties and chambers in the delegation.
In the coming days, the names of other legisdiants intending to engage are to be published.
He said the program is still being worked on.
Although Murphy said the goal of the trip is to help repair relations, he added that "well-intentioned educational measures" would also be prepared.
“European leaders need to be honest with their own people when it comes to espionage programs that they’ve been exploiting themselves for years.
"While we are adjusting our surveillance programs to better protect the rights of Europeans, they also have to realize the fact that we're not the only ones doing spy here," Murphy said.
In the meantime, Mr Kerry is going to the Middle East this weekend and in Poland to respond to animosity caused by U.S. strategies in Syria, Egypt and Iran, as well as U.S. follow-up activities.
